repl.append((92, "operationalised as areal BMD (a composite of volumetric density",
"OA is a disorder of the osteochondral unit [27\u201330], in which subchondral bone remodelling feeds cartilage degradation. A systemic, polygenic bone-forming set-point readable as areal BMD would protect at the whole-bone endpoint and raise OA risk at the joint endpoint \u2014 the Systemic Osteogenic Set-point (SOS [34]), operationalised as areal BMD (a composite of volumetric density and size) and used here as a heuristic organising the predictions below, not a fitted model. If the causal quantity is volumetric density, an areal-BMD estimate is diluted by skeletal size, so volumetric-BMD (or area-conditioned) instruments should yield a BMD\u2192OA effect at least as large as the areal one \u2014 the quantitative form of F2, which the present areal data cannot test. Height captures neither bone width nor geometry [35], so the height-independent 67\u201391% residual is not established as bone mass (resolved only by F2). The reading generates six predictions (P1\u2013P6) and seven falsifying tests (F1\u2013F7; Table S11): P1 supported, P2 tested here (systemic clause supported, site-ordering under-powered), P3 directionally consistent but not discriminating, P4 supported subject to the mass-versus-size split that only F2 resolves, P6 restated as a magnitude claim; F1 pre-registered. Two tests are executed here for the first time. F6 (cross-site readout homogeneity) found no heterogeneity among the five site-specific BMD\u2192knee-OA estimates (Q=6.92, 4 df, p=0.14, \u03c4\u00b2=0, shared-instrument covariance handled exactly) and is therefore not rejected, but discriminates little (with five estimates and site-mean r_g 0.69\u20130.96 a systemic set-point and correlated site-specific processes both predict homogeneity). F7 (joint-locus exclusion) found GDF5 not genome-wide-significant for BMD at any site, and excluding WNT16 or SOX6 changes no estimate's direction or significance."))

repl.append((98, "calibrated against GEFOS (OR 1.40\u20131.42 vs 1.55)",
"**Strengths and limitations.** Strengths: ten BMD sites with two OA definitions, an internal BMD\u2013fracture positive control calibrated against GEFOS (OR 1.40\u20131.42 vs 1.55), dual FDR/Bonferroni correction, a common SNP set, and directional concordance across IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median and MR-PRESSO. Limitations: the BMD instruments are European-only whereas the knee-OA outcome is 87.31% multi-ancestry (Table S14) and LDSC uses a European-only reference, so the forward estimates generalise most directly to European-ancestry populations (the near-1.0 intercept does not exclude ancestry-mismatch bias). Five of ten prespecified BMD sites entered MR because strict clumping completed for them; the five deferred sites showed comparable genome-wide signal, so the analysed subset reflects compute availability rather than biology (Table S12). Areal BMD conflates size with mass [18,32,37]; height-conditioning bounds the size share at 9.5\u201333.2% (a lower bound), leaving the 67\u201391% height-independent residual unresolved into bone mass versus geometry (test F2), and because conditioning was on height not BMI a BMI-mediated component is not excluded [35]. All estimates are sex-averaged and effect modification by sex is untested (deposited statistics are not sex-stratified). Heterogeneity is high across all pairs including the positive control, so the causal claim rests on estimator concordance rather than fixed-effect p-values; the reverse and OA\u2192fracture estimates are null but weakly powered (0.14\u20130.26, excluding only |\u03b2|\u22650.30), and residual overlap is bounded at \u22642.6% for BMD\u2192OA but cannot be removed with summary data. The tests executed here (F6, F7) separate the set-point from site-specific and joint-locus rivals but not from a generic systemic-pleiotropy rival, requiring the external-data tests F2\u2013F5."))

repl.append((52, "20\u201335 instruments retained per analysed BMD site, 116 for knee OA)",
"**MR.** Instruments were selected at p<5\u00d710\u207b\u2078 and clumped at r\u00b2<0.001 over 10 Mb against 1000 Genomes Phase 3 EUR (strict PLINK-equivalent clumping benchmarked against the PLINK binary; 20\u201335 instruments retained per analysed BMD site, 116 for knee OA). The BMD exposure set followed a prespecified rule: of the 11 DXA-derived BMD sites reported by Qian et al. 2025, the cranial head site \u2014 the only non-load-bearing region, whose instruments carry non-skeletal pleiotropy (head BMD is genetically correlated with intracranial aneurysm in the source GWAS [2]) \u2014 was excluded a priori, leaving ten prespecified canonical DXA regions analysed under the same rule: a site enters MR only if strict clumping completes. Clumping completed for five sites at submission (arms, femoral neck, lumbar spine, legs, total body), each yielding \u226515 independent instruments; the remaining five showed comparable genome-wide signal (229\u2013345 loci at p<5\u00d710\u207b\u2078 each) but their strict clumping was not completed in the compute environment (1000G EUR panel unavailable) and were deferred rather than analysed with a non-prespecified instrument set (Table S12). Instrument shortage drove no exclusion. Harmonisation against the outcome GWAS reduced the analysed sets to 16\u201331 instruments per forward pair and 69 per knee-OA-exposure pair; 47 of the 116 knee-OA instruments (41%) were lost (Fig. S9), an attrition we report explicitly. The 69 retained instruments were identical across all three knee-OA outcomes, so loss was uniform; the dropped instruments were not effect-size selected (median \u2212log10P 9.10 vs 9.89 retained; 43% carried stronger signal)."))

repl.append((88, "hip-versus-knee contrast explicable under joint-specific pathway dominance",
"Zhou et al. [5] reported higher femoral-neck BMD protected against hip OA, whereas our ten-site analysis shows higher BMD raises knee-OA risk \u2014 a hip-versus-knee contrast explicable under joint-specific pathway dominance (hip OA more mechanically/morphologically driven, knee OA more by systemic bone-formation pathways), cautioning against generalising any single BMD\u2192OA estimate across joints. Hartley et al. [22] reported a positive BMD effect on both hip (OR 1.28, 1.05\u20131.57) and knee OA (OR 1.40, 1.20\u20131.63); Funck-Brentano et al. [36] likewise reported a positive overall effect (all-OA OR 1.14, 1.06\u20131.22), significant at both knee and hip; a quantitative comparison on the native scales is in Fig. S12. These contradict the directional form of prediction P6, motivated by Zhou's protective femoral-neck finding; P6 and falsification rule F1 are in Table S11 and adjudicated in \u00a74. We adjudicate rather than cite selectively. First, the hip evidence disagrees with itself (protective [5], harmful [22], [36]). Second, decisively for F1, the criterion is conjunctive: the hip effect must be positive AND of knee-like magnitude. Within Hartley et al., the only same-pipeline comparison, the hip and knee intervals overlap almost completely (1.05\u20131.57 vs 1.20\u20131.63) and on the log-odds scale the hip estimate lies 27% below the knee's (0.247 vs 0.336); Hartley establishes positivity, not equality. We therefore restate P6 as a magnitude claim and pre-register the decision rule (a hip effect positive and at least as large as the knee's would fail P6); the directional form was pre-registered, its restatement here deviates from that form and is prompted by the contradictory hip evidence, and the restated form scores only prospectively collected hip estimates."))

repl.append((68, "total-body \u03b2=+0.023 (p=3.9\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2070)",
"MR supported a positive effect of BMD on knee OA across all five exposures (Table 2): total-body \u03b2=+0.023 (p=3.9\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2070), lumbar-spine +0.028 (p=7.7\u00d710\u207b\u2079), arms +0.022 (p=1.7\u00d710\u207b\u2076), legs +0.018 (p=2.3\u00d710\u207b\u2075), femoral-neck +0.017 (p=6.4\u00d710\u207b\u2074). On the odds-ratio scale the effect is small individually \u2014 OR 1.017\u20131.028 per SD higher BMD across the five sites, per-site 95% CIs in Table 2 \u2014 against OR 1.40 (1.20\u20131.63) per SD in the largest one-sample estimate [22], a 12\u201320-fold log-odds difference (adjudicated \u00a74). MR-Egger intercepts were all p>0.24 and weighted medians matched the IVW sign at all five sites, with bootstrap 95% CIs excluding the null at three of five; with 16\u201331 instruments the intercept test has limited power, so a non-significant intercept excludes unbalanced pleiotropy of detectable size rather than pleiotropy itself. Leave-one-out produced no sign reversals and directions agreed with r_g (+0.12 to +0.25). Heterogeneity was high (I\u00b2=68\u201386%); under random-effects IVW only two of five exposures remained significant (total-body p=0.006, lumbar-spine p=0.017; a DerSimonian\u2013Laird additive-\u03c4\u00b2 implementation additionally retains arms, p=0.033), so the count depends on the random-effects convention and we base the causal reading on estimator concordance rather than fixed-effect p-values. All five effects remained positively causal after MR-PRESSO outlier removal, with four of five strengthening. The positive control was comparably heterogeneous (I\u00b2 78% and 83%), indicating high I\u00b2 is a property of the BMD instrument sets rather than an OA-specific IVW failure; a multi-estimator robustness overview of all ten pairs is in Supplementary Fig. S11."))

repl.append((90, "0.336 on the log-odds scale \u2014 against 0.017\u20130.028 here",
"The directional agreement with Hartley et al. [22] is matched by a magnitude disagreement we state rather than leave to be discovered: their knee estimate is OR 1.40 (1.20\u20131.63) per SD higher eBMD \u2014 0.336 on the log-odds scale \u2014 against 0.017\u20130.028 here, a 12\u201320-fold difference. Three features make this a difference of scale and design rather than measured biology. First, the exposures differ: Hartley instrumented heel-ultrasound eBMD, a raw attenuation measure scaling with bone size, whereas we instrumented DXA areal BMD, area-normalised, so the two per-SD effects are not denominated in a common unit. Second, the designs differ: Hartley's headline estimate is one-sample two-stage least squares on unweighted allele scores in the same individuals as the outcome (overlap 100% by construction), whereas ours is bounded at \u22642.6% and an MRlap correction moved every estimate a median 20.5% further from the null. Third, our pipeline is calibrated: the BMD\u2192fracture positive control returns OR 1.40\u20131.42 against GEFOS 1.55, so a systematic deflation would have to be specific to the OA outcome. Inverting Hartley's reverse estimate onto the forward scale (\u03b2 4.16 SD per doubling of knee-OA risk, implying OR \u22481.18 per SD higher eBMD) leaves a residual gap of roughly five-fold, so the discrepancy is not one-sample artefact alone. We read the directions as concordant and the magnitudes as unresolved, and record the disagreement as a limitation."))

repl.append((94, "Two boundaries limit what these predictions can discriminate",
"Two boundaries limit what these predictions can discriminate. First, the tests executed here separate the set-point architecture from site-specific, local-mechanical and joint-locus rivals, but not from a generic systemic rival in which any BMD-raising process also acts at the joint; that separation requires F2\u2013F5 (volumetric decomposition, tissue enrichment, eQTL colocalisation, osteophyte-stratified outcome), unavailable with the present data. Second, P2's site-ordering clause is under-powered as a pairwise contrast (arms minus femoral neck +0.005, one-sided p=0.18), and P3 is not discriminating (a null colocalisation is predicted under any diffuse mechanism); one of ten pairwise contrasts (lumbar spine versus legs, p=0.022) reaches nominal significance \u2014 chance-compatible under Bonferroni but not excluded. Among the pre-registered predictions the genuinely discriminating one is P4, whose residual is resolved only by F2. The lowest-cost, highest-yield test is F5, pre-registered here: re-run with the OA outcome stratified into osteophyte-predominant versus joint-space-narrowing-predominant subtypes and test whether the BMD\u2192OA estimate concentrates in the former; because osteophytes are the structural signature of the set-point, failure to concentrate would falsify the mechanism and concentration would confirm it against the same fracture positive control. F5 needs an OA GWAS with radiographic subtypes and is pre-registered rather than performed, so we claim no result from it."))

repl.append((54, "**Sample overlap.** All pairs share UK Biobank participants",
"**Sample overlap.** All pairs share UK Biobank participants, partially violating the two-sample assumption. Under the Burgess approximation [14], the IVW bias equals the overlap fraction \u00d7 r_g, bounded above by 2.6% for BMD\u2192OA and 12% for BMD\u2192fracture, and acts in the direction of r_g; because r_g shares the sign of the MR effect in all ten pairs (as does the SNP-effect correlation \u03c1_GE, Fig. 9), this component acts away from the null. The two other IVW biases \u2014 weak-instrument (NOME) attenuation and winner's curse from selecting genome-wide-significant instruments \u2014 act towards the null. We report MR-Egger, weighted median and MR-PRESSO alongside IVW and applied MRlap [15], which simultaneously corrects weak-instrument bias and winner's curse while accounting for participant overlap, to all ten pairs. The joint correction moved every estimate further from the null (median +20.5%; observed-versus-corrected difference p<0.05 for 9/10 pairs), showing that the towards-null components dominate the away-from-null overlap component, and it reversed no direction or significance conclusion. The primary IVW estimates are therefore conservative with respect to the combined bias, but not with respect to overlap alone; we report MRlap alongside, rather than instead of, the primary IVW."))

repl.append((64, "SNP-based heritability ranged 0.26\u20130.39 across the ten BMD sites",
"SNP-based heritability ranged 0.26\u20130.39 across the ten BMD sites and was 0.035 (knee OA), 0.022 (self-reported OA) and 0.026 (fracture); 12/13 LDSC intercepts fell between 1.01 and 1.07 (fracture 0.96). Because LDSC uses a European-only reference while the knee-OA GWAS is multi-ancestry (Table S14), an intercept near 1.0 for knee OA does not exclude population-stratification or ancestry-mismatch bias and is not read as 'little stratification'; the ancestry mismatch is a primary limitation (\u00a74). The 13\u00d713 r_g matrix showed three blocks (Figure 1, Table 2). The ten BMD sites inter-correlated strongly (r_g 0.69\u20130.96, mean 0.81). Both OA definitions correlated positively with all ten BMD sites (knee OA +0.14 to +0.19; self-reported OA +0.12 to +0.25; 20/20 significant) and with each other (r_g=+0.84). The two definitions also agreed at the variant level: across 1,980 genome-wide-significant knee-OA loci, 94.4% had concordant signed Z with self-reported OA, confirming the two definitions relate to bone in the same genetic direction (OA_selfrep validation). Any-fracture correlated negatively with every BMD site (r_g \u22120.45 to \u22120.60; all q_FDR<0.001) and positively but non-significantly with both OA definitions (+0.15, +0.21). Of 78 pairs, 76 were significant at BH-FDR and 75 at Bonferroni; the two OA\u2013fracture pairs were the expected null."))

repl.append((78, "At WNT16, SOX6 and GDF5, loci nominated as shared effectors",
"At WNT16, SOX6 and GDF5, loci nominated as shared effectors, colocalisation with knee OA was not supported (PP.H4 \u22640.005 at each; e.g. WNT16 knee-OA z=0.94, p=0.35, PP.H4=0.002; GDF5 knee-OA z=\u221213.7 but no BMD genome-wide signal within \u00b1500 kb, PP.H4 0.003\u20130.017). Across all 31 BMD instrument loci, none reached PP.H4\u22650.8 (median 0.005); six favoured distinct variants (PP.H3\u22650.8). One locus fell marginally short: rs11228269 (chr11:68.29 Mb, LRP5 region; BMD z=\u22128.65, knee-OA z=\u22124.06, direction-concordant) gave PP.H4=0.799, 0.0006 below the pre-specified threshold, with residual posterior mass on a BMD-only signal (PP.H1=0.156). We retain the pre-specified threshold (the posterior is continuous and the 0.8 cut-off is a convention; a 0.75 threshold would read this locus as shared) and do not count this as colocalisation. The pipeline detected colocalisation where expected: at WNT16, BMD versus fracture gave PP.H4=0.999\u20131.000. The knee-OA null reflects power \u2014 under the MR estimate the expected knee-OA z at the strongest instrument is 0.85, and reaching genome-wide significance would require ~5\u00d710\u2077 participants (~42\u00d7 the present sample)."))

repl.append((60, "**MVMR.** Multivariable MR conditioned the BMD\u2192OA effect on adult height",
"**MVMR.** Multivariable MR conditioned the BMD\u2192OA effect on adult height (Yengo et al. 2022, N=5.0M) [19] as a skeletal-size proxy, using the MVMR-IVW estimator [20] and MVMR-Egger [21], judged by the Sanderson\u2013Smith conditional F (Fc\u226510). The primary configuration requested up to n_h=100 height instruments (49\u201350 survived harmonisation; Yengo et al. 2022); a grid (n_h \u2208 {0,100,300,1,000}) and both EUR and EUR_noUKB height sources are in the Supplementary Material. Height was chosen over BMI deliberately: BMI is itself a major knee-OA risk factor and part of the adiposity pathway through which a BMD-raising process could raise knee-OA risk, whereas height indexes skeletal size without adiposity. The consequence is stated explicitly (\u00a74): this analysis does not condition on BMI, so the height-independent residual may still contain a BMI-mediated component. A BMI-conditioned multivariable analysis is the natural extension and requires BMI summary statistics that are not part of this deposit."))

repl.append((36, "Genome-wide data can separate these explanations",
"Genome-wide data can separate these explanations. LDSC estimates genetic correlation (r_g) from summary statistics, tolerates sample overlap, and is unbiased by stratification when the intercept is near unity [6]. Two-sample MR uses genetic variants as instruments to interrogate causality [13]. Applied to OA and BMD, both have given partial answers [16,17]: Hackinger et al. reported r_g=+0.18 between hip/knee OA and lumbar-spine BMD (p=0.022) [4]; Zhou et al. reported r_g=+0.13 by LDSC and +0.17 by high-definition likelihood between hip OA and femoral-neck BMD, supporting a negative femoral-neck BMD\u2192hip OA effect [5]; and a 2025 two-sample MR of BMD against OA progression reports the same site-by-joint dependence, femoral-neck BMD raising hip-replacement risk (OR 1.27, 95% CI 1.13\u20131.43) but not knee-replacement risk [31]. Our BMD\u2192knee-OA analysis, by contrast, finds a positive effect that contrasts with Zhou's negative femoral-neck estimate; the hip-versus-knee reversal is discussed below."))

repl.append((48, "**Data sources.** Thirteen GWAS summary-statistic datasets were analysed",
"**Data sources.** Thirteen GWAS summary-statistic datasets were analysed: radiographic knee OA (OA_knee; GO Consortium 2025, N\u22481.21M) [1], self-reported OA (OA_selfrep; IEU OpenGWAS `ukb-b-14486`, N=462,933; 38,472 cases) [3], ten regional BMD sites (Qian et al. 2025, N\u22481.873 each) [2], and any-fracture (GEFOS 2018, N=264,973; 37,857 cases) [10]; full accessions are in the Data availability statement. All source GWAS were approved by their original ethics committees and this secondary analysis required no new ethical approval. All four contribute sex-combined primary estimates and none is stratified by sex, so every estimate here is sex-averaged and effect modification by sex cannot be tested \u2014 a material limitation given that both BMD and OA are sex-dimorphic (\u00a74). All LDSC used 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European LD scores; the ancestry composition of each GWAS is in Table S14. Because that reference is European-only while the knee-OA GWAS is multi-ancestry (87.31% European; Table S14), the near-1.0 LDSC intercept cannot be read as excluding population-stratification bias, and the instrument\u2013outcome ancestry mismatch is a primary limitation (\u00a74)."))

